I'll add raw mode configuration. Note: it uses non-blocking reads (make_nonblock). For termios VMIN/VTIME with O_NONBLOCK, reads return EAGAIN when no data — ppp_arch_getchar handles that (returns 0).

Let me write the patch:

```c
static int open_tty(const char *dev)
{
  struct termios tio;
  int fd;
  int errcode;

  if ((fd = open(dev, O_RDWR)) < 0)
    {
      return fd;
    }

  /* Put the line into raw mode: PPP frames must not be echoed,
   * line-disciplined or expanded by the tty layer.
   */

  if (tcgetattr(fd, &tio) == OK)
    {
      cfmakeraw(&tio);
      tcsetattr(fd, TCSANOW, &tio);
    }

  if ((errcode = make_nonblock(fd)) < 0)
    {
      close(fd);
      return errcode;
    }

  debug_printf("tty fd:%i\n", fd);

  return fd;
}
```

Need `#include <termios.h>` in pppd.c. Let me check the includes.

Then pppd_main.c: ttyname "/dev/ttyS0" → "/dev/console".

Hmm, wait. Let me think about whether /dev/console is the right choice vs. also registering the console UART as ttyS0... 

Option A: pppd uses /dev/console (simple, works).
Option B: Fix the vendor_sifli UART registration so the console UART is ALSO registered as ttyS0 — but that could conflict (two device nodes for the same UART; also the README claims both exist, so maybe the intended behavior was to register both; the code comment says "Register all remaining UARTs" — deliberate choice).

Hmm, actually, for a cleaner long-term design, Option A is fine and minimal.

But hold on — one more consideration for the demo: with pppd on /dev/console and NSH asleep, everything works. But what if someone wants both console AND ppp? Same UART — can't split. Fine.

Let me make the patches.

Also, I should double check that after cfmakeraw, the console still works for writing (we need console output for logs) — raw mode disables OPOST (no \n → \r\n translation). Our terminal handles \n fine (earlier logs had \r\n; with OPOST off, \n alone might display oddly in my Python reader, but that's cosmetic).

Let me implement both patches now.

